comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-14T16:56:10.232Z","harness:claude-code","Root cause (2026-11-14): PmCompleteListValidationError built a fixed recovery (pm list ... --no-truncate ... and await client.listAllComplete()) with no knowledge of the request, so a refused includeBody request was retried without bodies. Fix: the constructor takes PmCompleteListOptions; certifyCompleteListResult and assertCompleteListResult take an optional options argument, and without it read the candidate's own filters.include_body record (every list envelope carries it when bodies were requested). listAllComplete passes its options through. The recovery drops the redundant legacy --no-truncate alias because --output-limit unbounded --output-budget unbounded already cover it."
  "2026-11-14T17:19:50.100Z","harness:claude-code","Correction (2026-11-14, same session): the recovery KEEPS --no-truncate. Executing the emitted retry without it against a fresh tracker returned 3/3 items with has_more false, yet certification refused it as page_incomplete, because inspectEnvelopeShape requires filters.no_truncate and pm list echoes that only for the legacy flag (src/sdk/query/list.ts); --no-truncate also lifts list's own default page limit (resolveListPageLimit caps an unlimited read at 20 rows on a tracker of 10,000+ items). So the alias is load-bearing, not redundant, and the issue's secondary request is declined with that evidence. The mismatch it exposes (the migration hint tells callers to replace --no-truncate with --output-limit unbounded --output-budget unbounded, which yields an uncertifiable list) is filed separately."
